description:
pkix-path-builder/src/lib.rs:185-194 (rustdoc on `Error::NoValidPath`):

```rust
/// `last_error` is the [`pkix_path::Error::Display`] rendering of the
/// last candidate's validation failure. …
///
/// [`SignatureVerifier`]: pkix_path::SignatureVerifier
/// [`pkix_path::Error::Display`]: pkix_path::Error
```

The intra-doc link `[`pkix_path::Error::Display`]` is a reference-style link with definition `pkix_path::Error::Display: pkix_path::Error`. Rustdoc resolves `pkix_path::Error` to the type page and emits an HTML anchor with visible text "pkix_path::Error::Display" pointing at `…/pkix_path/enum.Error.html`. A reader clicks the link expecting to see the `Display` impl and lands on the enum's main page.

This is technically a working link, so `cargo doc --all-features` won't warn (unlike PKIX-wnnn.2 where the target was a private item — that one's a hard fail). But the visible text mismatch is misleading: the reader has to scroll the Error type page to find the Display impl block, with no indication that's what was intended.

Three principled fixes (pick one):

1. **Drop the `::Display` from the link, mention Display in surrounding prose**:
   ```
   /// `last_error` is the [`Display`] rendering of the last candidate's
   /// [`pkix_path::Error`].
   ```
   Rustdoc links to `pkix_path::Error`, and `Display` is the standard trait — the reader recognizes the pattern.

2. **Use a fragment link to the actual Display impl block**: this is harder to make stable across rustdoc versions because the anchor ID is generated.

3. **Plain text, no link**:
   ```
   /// `last_error` is the `Display` rendering of the last
   /// [`pkix_path::Error`].
   ```

Option (3) is the cheapest. Option (1) is the most informative.

Distinct from PKIX-wnnn.2 (which is the `cargo doc` failure on a private link). This one is a UX/accuracy issue, not a build failure.

Note: this is "opinion-adjacent." If the maintainer prefers the current link as a stylistic choice (where the visible text describes intent, not target), close as opinion. But the current text-vs-target mismatch is real.
